## What this repository changed

**Renaming and metadata**

- Project name `WebHarvest` → `WebStudio` throughout (192 occurrences across
  81 files), including UI copy, page titles, log prefixes, cookie names
  (`webstudio_preview_id`, `webstudio_mock_session_token`) and postMessage
  event names (`webstudio:login_submit`).
- `components/ui/WebHarvestLogo.tsx` → `components/ui/WebStudioLogo.tsx`, with
  all three importing files updated.
- `package.json` name, version and description; `package-lock.json` root entry;
  `render.yaml` service name.

**Code changes**

- `app/api/github-stars/route.ts` rewritten: the upstream repository path was
  hard-coded. It now reads `GITHUB_REPO` from the environment and returns
  `{ "stars": null }` when unset, instead of reporting a misleading count.
  This is the only behavioural change to an endpoint.

**New features (not present upstream)**

- **Offline auth emulation** — `lib/authflow/detect.ts`,
  `lib/authflow/runtime.ts`, `GET /api/mirror/[id]/auth-flow`, the **Auth Flow**
  studio tab, and the injection hook in the preview route. A mirrored site's
  login and sign-up forms now work offline: a visitor registers or signs in
  against a browser-local account store, a session is established under the
  names the target app expects, and they are taken into the authenticated area.
- **Live proxy** — `app/api/mirror/[id]/live/[[...path]]/route.ts` and the
  **Snapshot / Live** switch in the preview panel. Snapshot serves captured
  files; Live reverse-proxies the real site on demand, rewriting HTML, CSS,
  redirects and `Set-Cookie` headers so the page renders and its forms — including
  the login form — submit to the real server.
- **Endpoint discovery** — `lib/analysis/endpoints.ts`,
  `GET /api/mirror/[id]/endpoints`, and the **Endpoints** studio tab. Merges the
  crawl manifest, the engine's API catalogue and string literals from captured
  scripts into one catalogue, surfacing routes the crawler never reached.
- **Branding** — the footer now links to this repository.

**Security hardening**

- **Access gate** — `lib/security/app-auth.ts`, `app/login/page.tsx` and
  `app/api/auth/access/route.ts`. A shared `WEBSTUDIO_ACCESS_TOKEN` is exchanged
  for a signed, HttpOnly session cookie, enforced for every route in
  `middleware.ts`. Production with no token fails closed with a 503.
- **CSRF** — `middleware.ts` rejects state-changing requests that arrive
  cross-site, judged by `Sec-Fetch-Site` and `Origin`.
- **SSRF fix** — `POST /api/mirror/auth` now runs the same URL validation, DNS
  guard and rate limiting as `POST /api/mirror`.
- **Encrypted profiles** — `lib/security/crypto.ts` seals stored authentication
  profiles with AES-256-GCM; plaintext rows keep working and migrate lazily.
- **Mock auth contained** — `/api/auth/session` and `/api/auth/[...nextauth]`
  now refuse requests that did not come from a mirror preview.
- **Deployment** — `render.yaml` corrected to Node 22, a realistic heap, a
  mounted disk and generated secrets; storage paths are now configurable via
  `WEBSTUDIO_DATA_DIR` and `WEBSTUDIO_DOWNLOAD_DIR`.
- **Preview route split** — MIME overrides, path resolution and the containment
  guard moved into `lib/preview/`.

**Additions**

- `docs/` — a full specification: architecture, crawl lifecycle, data model,
  security model including known gaps, a recreation guide, a structure map, an
  auth-emulation spec, an endpoint-discovery spec, a build guide written for an
  AI agent, and one document per API endpoint (27 routes plus the edge
  middleware).
- `LICENSE` — the MIT text, retaining the upstream copyright line.
- `NOTICE` — this file.
- `.env.example` — the supported environment variables.

## What was deliberately not changed

The crawling engine, preview route, security modules, processors, database
layer and UI components are carried over **unchanged apart from the rename**.
They were not refactored, because the behaviour of a 13,000-line crawler is
expensive to re-verify and a rewrite would have silently changed what the
product does. Where the original has sharp edges — the four-strategy dispatch,
the third-party template special-cases, the plaintext auth profiles — they are
documented in `docs/04-security.md` and `docs/05-recreation-guide.md` rather
than quietly removed.

